SUMMARY:The American Creed in Words and Images
LOCATION:Acadiana Center for the Arts\, 101 W. Vermilion St\, Lafayette
DESCRIPTION:For teachers of 6–12 grade\nAmerica’s 250th anniversary is 
 an ideal time to reflect on and explore the American Creed\, what economis
 t Gunnar Myrdal refers to as “ (what) Americans of all national origins\
 , classes\, regions\, creeds\, and colors\, have . . . in common: a social
  ethos\, a political creed.” Since 2018\, students around the country ha
 ve been discussing American Creed videos (streaming on PBS LearningMedia) 
 in class\, with curricular support from the National Writing Project. Stud
 ents have also been invited to create and share their own stories and refl
 ections in photos\, captions and/or essays via KQED’s national Youth Med
 ia Challenge publishing platform. This workshop supports adding Louisiana 
 voices\, which have been largely missing\, to this nationwide initiative. 
 Let’s get Acadiana students published!\nTwo National Writing Project Tea
 cher Consultants\, Lafayette Parish School System middle school teachers w
 ho have recently incorporated these exercises with their students\, and th
 e American Creed in Louisiana project lead\, will guide middle and high sc
 hool teachers through using photopoetry\, a combination of art and literat
 ure that expresses feelings and emotions through both words and images. Pa
 rticipants will learn to guide students to create their own photopoems tha
 t capture the limitless potential and challenges of our American Creed.\nN
 o prior poetry or photography experience is required to participate. Bring
  your pens and phones and immerse yourself in easily adaptable exercises t
 hat can immediately be incorporated into classrooms to engage students in 
 exploring these important foundational ideas of our nation in creative way
 s.\nABOUT THE PRESENTERS\nToby Daspit is Professor of Education and Emerit
 us Consultant with the National Writing Project of Acadiana at the Univers
 ity of Louisiana at Lafayette. He is the Project Lead for American Creed i
 n Louisiana\, funded by the National Writing Project. Toby has nearly four
  decades of experience as a middle\, high school\, and university educator
 . He is the founder of the Improving the Blank Page Youth Writing Program 
 and is a featured poet for the 2026 Festival of Words.\nEmily Weber is a m
 iddle school English Language Arts teacher at Edgar Martin Middle School i
 n Lafayette Parish. She is currently pursuing a Master’s degree in Educa
 tional Leadership at the University of Louisiana Monroe. Emily’s classro
 om is built around the belief that every student has a story worth telling
 . She has a particular passion for creative writing and strives to help st
 udents discover that writing is not just an academic skill but a powerful 
 way to communicate\, reflect\, and connect with the world around them. Emi
 ly is a National Writing Project Teacher Consultant.\nKerrie Skerrett teac
 hes gifted 6th grade English Language Arts at Edgar Martin Middle School a
 nd is entering her 14th year as an educator\, with experience teaching in 
 both general education and gifted settings. She is a graduate of both ULL 
 and ULM. Kerrie\, a National Writing Project Teacher Consultant\, is invol
 ved in the American Creed Project because she is passionate about giving s
 tudents opportunities to engage in authentic writing and make meaningful c
 onnections to the world around them.\n\nhttps://happeningnext.com/event/th
 e-american-creed-in-words-and-images-eid3a0e7lfooe
